# gtk3 -- GTK 3, built Wayland-only, and packaged for exactly one reason.
#
# THIS LOOKS WRONG IN A TREE WHOSE README SAYS "a complete GTK 4 application
# platform", so the reason is written here rather than left to be re-derived:
#
#   gnome-control-center 48.4's meson.build, lines 298-306, requires
#   libnma-gtk4 unconditionally under host_is_linux -- no required:false, and
#   meson_options.txt has NO network option at all, so no -D switch can drop
#   it. Without it, Settings does not configure networking.
#
#   libnma 1.10.6's meson.build line 159 has a TOP-LEVEL, UNCONDITIONAL
#   gtk+-3.0 dependency. -Dlibnma_gtk4=true does not replace that, it ADDS a
#   GTK 4 library beside it. So the GTK 4 network widgets that
#   gnome-control-center needs cannot be built without GTK 3 present.
#
# The README says "Wayland first", not "GTK 4 only" -- and it already packages
# the X client libraries while running no X server, for the same kind of reason.
# This is that policy applied once more, not an exception to it.
#
# BLFS 12.4, pinned here rather than in versions.env -- versions.env is
# regenerated wholesale from the LFS book by tools/pin-versions.sh.
#
# NOT ON download.gnome.org UNDER THE NAME YOU EXPECT: the module is `gtk`, not
# `gtk+`, even for the 3.24 series -- /sources/gtk+/3.24/ does not exist. The
# sha256 below was checked against upstream's own published
# gtk-3.24.50.sha256sum rather than only against the downloaded bytes, which is
# the difference between "what I fetched" and "what upstream released".
SRC_URL=https://download.gnome.org/sources/gtk/3.24/gtk-3.24.50.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=399118a5699314622165a11b769ea9b6ed68e037b6d46d57cfcf4851dec07529
SRC_DIR=gtk-3.24.50

# -Dx11_backend=true -Dwayland_backend=true. BOTH, AND THE FIRST VERSION OF
# THIS RECIPE SAID THE OPPOSITE FOR A REASON THAT WAS TRUE WHEN WRITTEN.
#
# It read: "THE POINT OF THE PACKAGE'S SHAPE. Both default to TRUE, so leaving
# either alone builds an X11 GTK 3 and links libX11, libXi, libXrandr and the
# rest into libgdk-3 -- on a system with no X server."
#
# That was correct for the consumer set this package had: libnma, and nothing
# else. GTK 3 was packaged for exactly one reason -- libnma's unconditional
# gtk+-3.0 dependency, which gnome-control-center needs -- and libnma is a
# Wayland client like everything else here.
#
# THE CONSUMER SET GREW. The session tier needs GTK 3 WITH X11:
#   gnome-settings-daemon  requires gtk+-x11-3.0 BY NAME (meson.build 107), and
#                          that .pc file exists only when this backend is built
#   libcanberra-gtk3       includes gdk/gdkx.h; its earlier build failure was
#                          that header missing, which is this same backend
# Neither package existed here when the Wayland-only line was written. So the
# policy line "gtk3 serves one consumer and needs no X" is retired honourably
# rather than violated: the premise changed under it.
#
# The distinction is the same one gtk4's recipe now records: this tree does not
# RUN an X server, and it does have X CLIENTS, because Xwayland is packaged.
# GTK's X11 backend is client-side code.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, and it is less than either earlier flip in this cascade because
# both floors are already laid: cairo-xlib (shipped at cairo 1.18.4-2) and
# epoxy/glx.h (shipped at libepoxy 1.5.10-2, and gdk/x11/gdkglcontext-x11.h
# includes it here exactly as gtk4's does). Everything else -- x11, xext,
# xrender, xi, xcursor, xdamage, xfixes, xrandr, fontconfig -- is packaged and
# earlier in the build order. No new package.
#
# post-install.sh asserts the OUTCOME rather than the flag, and its expected
# value CHANGED with this commit: gtk+-3.0.pc's `targets=` must now read
# "wayland x11" where it previously had to read "wayland" alone.
#
# -Dbroadway_backend=false is already the default and is named so it stays a
# decision; it is an HTTP/websocket display backend and nothing here wants one.
#
# -Dxinerama=no. THE FLAG SURVIVES THE X11 FLIP; ITS REASON DOES NOT, so the
# reason is replaced rather than left to look still-valid.
#
# It said xinerama "is meaningless without the X11 backend, so it would be an X
# dependency acquired for a backend that is not built." The backend is now
# built, and that sentence is now false.
#
# It stays off on different grounds: XRandR SUPERSEDES IT AND IS ALREADY A HARD
# DEPENDENCY HERE. meson.build 557 takes xrandr >= 1.2.99 with no required:false
# whenever x11 is on, and RandR 1.2+ is how GTK enumerates monitors; Xinerama is
# the legacy path for servers too old to have it, and Xwayland is not one.
# Turning it on would add a library to serve a fallback that cannot be reached.
#
# -Dcolord=no. Also 'auto', and colord belongs to another chain. THE PIN IS
# UNCHANGED AND THE SENTENCE EXPLAINING IT WAS WRONG WITHIN THE HOUR, which is
# recorded here rather than quietly rewritten because it is the exact hazard
# the pin exists for.
#
# It said: "Today it resolves off because colord is absent; the moment that
# package lands, an unpinned auto would silently add a dependency to a rebuild
# of THIS package." colord published at 18:59 the same evening -- twenty-four
# minutes after this package first published -- so "colord is absent" became
# false immediately, and an unpinned auto WOULD now acquire it. The prediction
# was right and the factual claim beside it rotted.
#
# The word was also imprecise: a REBUILD seeds from the INDEX, so what matters
# is when a package PUBLISHES, not when it merges. Build order is decided by
# what has merged; seeding is decided by what has published, and this sentence
# is about seeding.
#
# Nothing here asks for colour management, so it stays off.
#
# -Dprint_backends=file,lpr. The default 'auto' is worse than it looks:
# modules/printbackends/meson.build line 20 sets print_strict_deps=false for
# auto, so a backend whose dependencies are missing is SILENTLY DROPPED, while
# an explicit list makes a missing dependency a hard error. cups is another
# chain's package; with 'auto', gtk3 would quietly gain a cups print backend
# the first time it was rebuilt after cups PUBLISHED (not merged -- a
# rebuild seeds from the index). `file` is mandatory on unix
# (line 37 errors without it) and `lpr` only spawns the lpr command, so neither
# adds a dependency.
#
# -Ddemos=false -Dexamples=false -Dtests=false. All three default TRUE. They
# build gtk3-demo, gtk3-widget-factory and a large test suite that nothing here
# runs; demos and examples also install binaries into /usr/bin.
#
# -Dgtk_doc=false -Dman=false are the defaults, named because both would need
# tooling that is not packaged.
#
# -Dintrospection=true is the default and is required: libnma builds GObject
# introspection data against GTK 3, and gnome-control-center reaches it through
# that. Declaring it means declaring gobject-introspection AND glib-introspection
# -- the scanner and the typelibs it reads are different packages.
MESON_ARGS="-Dx11_backend=true -Dwayland_backend=true -Dbroadway_backend=false \
-Dxinerama=no -Dcolord=no -Dprint_backends=file,lpr \
-Dcloudproviders=false -Dtracker3=false -Dprofiler=false \
-Ddemos=false -Dexamples=false -Dtests=false -Dinstalled_tests=false \
-Dgtk_doc=false -Dman=false -Dintrospection=true"
